     The default shell on OSX is bash, and the documentation
for it contains info about environment variables.  Overall,
it is probably simpler to maintain a configuration file.
The contents are plain text, so you need to use an editor
which doesn't insist on putting in mark-up stuff for formatting.

     Another post showed what the contents typically look like,
and mentioned ~/.nethackrc as the file.  On OSX, if nethack
can't find a file of that name, it tries again looking for
~/Library/Preferences/NetHack Defaults
as second choice (note the space in the file name and the
capitalization, particularly of the H in "NetHack"), and if it
can't find that either, the last chance is
~/Library/Preferences/NetHack Defaults.txt
(Apparently the trailing ".txt" helps persuade some editors
that the contents should be plain text and/or prevents them
from tacking on their own file name suffix when saving.  "~" is
shell shorthand for your home directory.)

